#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106651} :activity/id #uuid "eb407cdf-9aea-5548-a574-970269d4ce5b" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048184 :topic/id #uuid "86927b03-ba99-5587-9673-31a5bae6ef0b" :topic/math-academy-id 1317} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106651 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107178 :tutorial/content "The shape below consists of $6$ line segments defined by the $6$ points $A$, $B$, $C$, $D$, $E$, and $F.$ These line segments form a loop. \n\n![](images/25/259bfd166a7870bd1a057b28d4b3324f3b98d05222596f1426afa01d6573e3d8.png)\n\nA chain of straight line segments that form a loop is called a **polygon**.\n\nThe line segments are the **sides** of the polygon, and the endpoints of the sides are the **vertices**.\n\nFor this polygon:\n\n- The $6$ vertices are $A$, $B$, $C$, $D$, $E$, and $F.$\n\n- The $6$ sides are $\\overline{AB}$, $\\overline{BC}$, $\\overline{CD}$, $\\overline{DE}$, $\\overline{EF}$, and $\\overline{AF}.$\n\nTo denote a particular polygon, we start at one vertex and loop around the polygon, reading off the vertices in order. So, the polygon above can be denoted $ABCDEF.$\n\nThe singular form of the word *vertices* is **vertex**. So, $A$ is a vertex, $B$ is a vertex, etc." :tutorial/id #uuid "342100ff-c1a7-531c-90a6-e07a80e83fda" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9674 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "5fbe01e1-2066-5d27-b53a-a2fa74fc43e6" :step/math-academy-id 188116 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106652}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106652 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107179 :tutorial/content "The name of a polygon is determined by the number of sides it has. \n\nThe polygon below has $6$ sides. A six-sided polygon is called a **hexagon**.\n\n![](images/25/259bfd166a7870bd1a057b28d4b3324f3b98d05222596f1426afa01d6573e3d8.png)\n\nThe names of the polygons with up to ten sides are given below:\n\n| Number of sides | Name |\n| --- | --- |\n| $3$ | Triangle |\n| $4$ | Quadrilateral |\n| $5$ | Pentagon |\n| $6$ | Hexagon |\n| $7$ | Heptagon |\n| $8$ | Octagon |\n| $9$ | Nonagon |\n| $10$ | Decagon |" :tutorial/id #uuid "9aaf4a28-03c1-57ea-adcd-968b00ad8a41" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9675 :tutorial/title "Naming Polygons"} :step/id #uuid "d4d97dfe-283b-5019-b2e4-5d6ab6550b1d" :step/math-academy-id 188117 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106653}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106653 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105754 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105491 :question/id #uuid "3f0d9dbb-448f-5e1e-b06c-4b6a3a674b09" :question/math-academy-id "e-15616"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b9fd1d3c-8479-5b35-abbc-9664cea2e1a8" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049350}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106192} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106195} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442724} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442725} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442727}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Polygon"} :step/id #uuid "5e398f9a-e437-5d98-bf9e-cb85ba65689b" :step/math-academy-id 188118 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106654}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106654 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107182 :tutorial/content "We can categorize polygons in different ways.\n\nA polygon is **simple** if its boundary does not intersect itself. Otherwise, the polygon is **complex**.\n\n![](images/71/71ef7dbf87cb3a097ec153eb4c4e18920c2567e6a239a788f4340a0a5f33ea6f.png)\n\nA polygon is **convex** if it has no angles pointing inwards. Otherwise, if the polygon has angles pointing inwards, we call it **concave**.\n\n![](images/ef/ef7357c0e21760c3e7f159c253c527e35aac869ce16381132a53356dc53455e2.png)\n\nIt might help to remember that a con**cave** polygon looks a bit like the entrance to a **cave** when viewed from the outside. \n\n![](images/11/11e9844e49a5c586ec573e08865eb7d02dd68459e90df90749116a9eb1575430.png)\n\n**Note**: A more technical way to define a convex polygon is to say that all of its *internal angles* are less than $180^\\circ.$ If you're not sure what an internal angle is yet, don't worry! We'll learn more about the internal angles of a polygon in future lessons." :tutorial/id #uuid "b7c27ce6-fd4a-5560-bc80-0e72700ff320" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9678 :tutorial/title "Types of Polygons"} :step/id #uuid "e39203ba-5fa0-58f6-9ad6-4003cf7c5c77" :step/math-academy-id 188121 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106655}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106655 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105755 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105494 :question/id #uuid "d56604cb-e46a-5d52-94c4-dea5e8c0df3b" :question/math-academy-id "e-15623"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0d25a77b-7977-50b2-b375-51b8539bc9b9" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106215} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106216} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442716} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442718} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442719} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442722}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Type of a Polygon"} :step/id #uuid "ac8b8e7b-3a84-5a11-ab36-35cd5a021aa5" :step/math-academy-id 188122 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106656}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106656 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107180 :tutorial/content "Consider the pentagon $ABCDE,$ shown below.\n\n![](images/b1/b11b509e881cd813ff6879a0a6fa9559d51ffcbc5da4c150a0eaf0a9ee82a159.png)\n\nWe have the following definitions: \n\n- Two *sides* are **adjacent** (or **consecutive**) if they have a common vertex.\n\nFor example, $\\color[rgb]{0.753,0.494,0.643}\\overline{BC}$ is adjacent to $\\color[rgb]{0.902, 0.624, 0}\\overline{AB}$ *and* ${\\color[rgb]{0.902, 0.624, 0}\\overline{CD}}.$\n\n![](images/76/760a9cbccd117214d4e93392cdae17bf117497501b05f7b2e25c6c8cb5cd43e1.png)\n\n- Two *vertices* are **adjacent** if there is a side joining them. \n\nFor example, the vertex $E$ is adjacent to the vertices $D$ and $A$.\n\n![](images/6d/6d857920609da14fb0b31f762400c5f54e9e428717c2b55fcb17215280bbbfed.png)\n\n- A **diagonal** is a line segment that joins two non-adjacent vertices. \n\nFor example, ${\\color[rgb]{0.902, 0.624, 0}{\\overline{AC}}}, {\\color[rgb]{0.902, 0.624, 0}{\\overline{BD}}},$ and ${\\color[rgb]{0.902, 0.624, 0}{\\overline{BE}}}$ are diagonals of our pentagon.\n\n![](images/ad/adf7afaa66144a33dba7ff3732b01619bc13cac0fae59c466393d4084c748a59.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "b82f1900-5686-5fcc-8354-6c00b102f576" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9676 :tutorial/title "The Parts of a Polygon"} :step/id #uuid "0d4447dd-331d-5e2c-9e2b-470035c668cb" :step/math-academy-id 188125 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106657}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106657 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105756 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105492 :question/id #uuid "e72f5a3b-8085-54dc-a09a-121925ef509c" :question/math-academy-id "e-15617"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "61c58110-9813-5588-81d2-5735718589aa" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106198} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106387} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442720} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442721} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442726} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442730} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442731} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527176} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536825}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Adjacent Vertices and Sides"} :step/id #uuid "a35aa384-e796-5e75-adfa-23d8efbf0c06" :step/math-academy-id 188126 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106658}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106658 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105757 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105493 :question/id #uuid "33bf6630-1558-50b9-8823-0bd58d0787af" :question/math-academy-id "e-15618"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1ed0dc2a-7c76-5fee-9aa8-aad529cf1952" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106196} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106386} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106389} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442717} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442723} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442728} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442729}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Parts of a Polygon"} :step/id #uuid "94356407-759e-59ef-b5e4-6f11d01f5cf9" :step/math-academy-id 188129}] :activity/title "Polygons" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105971}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180602} :activity/id #uuid "e6fba530-9f3d-5ad2-a58a-2010478f12bf" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048392 :topic/id #uuid "9b9bc1e8-8910-5e7b-b200-0bc492f56034" :topic/math-academy-id 3719} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180602 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180768 :tutorial/content "Let's remind ourselves of the three rules for dividing with negative numbers:\n\n- A negative number divided by a positive number gives a negative number. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-)\\,÷\\,(+)\\,=\\,(-)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- A positive number divided by a negative number is also a negative number. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(+)\\,÷\\,(-)\\,=\\,(-)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- A negative number divided by a negative number is a positive number.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-)\\,÷\\,(-)\\,=\\,(+)\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "19bbbf1e-a31e-54b1-92bb-a2fb71bf54b8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8022 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "b2427cba-ac8b-55c2-95b7-2a0f2be0812b" :step/math-academy-id 53469 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180603}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180603 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180769 :tutorial/content "As an example, let's find the value of\n\n$$\n2.4 \\div (-4).\n$$\n\nDividing a positive number by a negative number gives a negative number. Hence, the result will be negative.\n\nTherefore, since \n\n$$\n2.4\\div 4=0.6,\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n2.4 \\div (-4) = -0.6.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "3c2ea5e2-fa57-573b-8e38-712b30958ca4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8023 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example"} :step/id #uuid "c0f81736-6a5b-5d18-9f29-8ef5290d319e" :step/math-academy-id 53470 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180604}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180604 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179798 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179445 :question/id #uuid "4fc788a3-fcac-5de0-a92b-8e818ce41160" :question/math-academy-id "e-12799"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "95cfce29-7f40-5b15-b299-363f8647e111" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048158} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049391} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050719} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050934}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180048} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180312} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479094} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479128}] :knowledge-point/title "Dividing With Negative Decimals"} :step/id #uuid "78adffff-c4c8-53d4-8be1-0adce0ba9c40" :step/math-academy-id 53471 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180605}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180605 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179799 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179446 :question/id #uuid "723f1bda-4e94-502f-aaea-d9a6c1259fd3" :question/math-academy-id "e-12800"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "57266531-c68d-57d9-b136-9d100dcc983e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049046} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049357}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180046} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180047} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479072} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479122} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483354} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536819}] :knowledge-point/title "Dividing With Negative Whole Numbers and Fractions"} :step/id #uuid "51cb3a96-772b-5cf8-a320-e21f950a928a" :step/math-academy-id 53474 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180606}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180606 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179800 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179447 :question/id #uuid "4cdf5132-1efc-52c2-8ec7-f26ef6ac833a" :question/math-academy-id "e-12801"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "15f1aff4-68c4-5acf-9de0-ad34e321ef3f" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049616}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180044} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180045} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479064} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479087} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483358} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483393}] :knowledge-point/title "Dividing With Negative Fractions"} :step/id #uuid "01563470-15f3-5ad3-a0d7-68cf371ee42d" :step/math-academy-id 53477}] :activity/title "Dividing With Negative Decimals and Fractions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179870}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046648} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483457} :activity/id #uuid "5fe34547-15c9-5b67-8853-ec0046fda80a" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048392 :topic/id #uuid "9b9bc1e8-8910-5e7b-b200-0bc492f56034" :topic/math-academy-id 3719} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483446 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483354 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9ee4d24e-23ef-5ad1-93ac-34b77b84473c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483080}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483080} :answer-field/id #uuid "b44317ba-2764-5aaf-a2ac-d25521c85278" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483310}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2ad85a11-bba5-5fae-afa8-bc7f058da66c" :question/math-academy-id "q-173783" :question/problem "Expressed as an improper fraction in its simplest form,  $(-6)÷\\frac{12}{5}=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Dividing a negative number by a positive number gives a negative number. Hence, the result will be negative.\n\nTherefore, since \n\n$$\n6÷\\frac{12}{5}=\\frac{6\\times 5}{12}=\\frac{30}{12}=\\frac{5}{2},\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n(-6)÷\\frac{12}{5}=-\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "2473d2dd-c36e-59cc-a24d-7cd36087675c"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483457 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483358 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15ba5d73-cfbf-5d35-89f4-a709f6e9f4ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{9}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482852} {:answer/id #uuid "2c669c68-aefe-5e7c-b2c5-c63d2b25d4ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482885} {:answer/id #uuid "3c34cd61-57c9-53a6-a30c-553a2947ad18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482915} {:answer/id #uuid "700bdd4a-0eec-5a10-a0a6-451aa0f53abd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483006} {:answer/id #uuid "76a67f28-627f-53d2-bf6b-8d0da5300ff8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483019}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482852} :answer-field/id #uuid "3589368a-bcb8-5009-b31d-b69be611aa69" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483255}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "35226f58-033d-50fc-9ed5-94472de795ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-128060" :question/problem "Find the value of $(-\\frac{3}{8})÷\\frac{1}{6}.$" :question/worked-solution "Dividing a negative number by a positive number gives a negative number. Hence, the result will be negative.\n\nTherefore, since \n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{8}÷\\frac{1}{6}=\\frac{3\\times 6}{8\\times 1}=\\frac{18}{8}=\\frac{9}{4},\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n(-\\frac{3}{8})÷\\frac{1}{6}=-\\frac{9}{4}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "4a47f131-26dd-585f-ba85-92f4312d0160" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483502}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483502 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483393 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "424b2dd8-9b73-548c-b7cc-4ca500b6b156" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482924} {:answer/id #uuid "6f8353d3-9579-5c1e-8802-4ea1bdff86fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483004} {:answer/id #uuid "ab0fb722-71f0-5f53-8b20-486ab4bacd00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483102} {:answer/id #uuid "cd695a82-2dbd-573c-8f41-021965ec84e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483154} {:answer/id #uuid "ce613343-8043-5bc1-ae12-aad3ccc46878" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483158}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483004} :answer-field/id #uuid "7774d184-d47b-5051-8e81-b32b977ef33a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483285}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ab43471e-dafe-5dcf-b8bc-06037ed994ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-128062" :question/problem "Determine the value of $(-\\frac{1}{5})÷(-\\frac{3}{10}).$" :question/worked-solution "Dividing a negative number by a negative number gives a positive number. Hence, the result will be positive.\n\nTherefore, since \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{5}÷\\frac{3}{10}=\\frac{1\\times 10}{5\\times 3}=\\frac{10}{15}=\\frac{2}{3},\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n(-\\frac{1}{5})÷(-\\frac{3}{10})=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "e5cfbbd4-d11e-5405-ba15-8c0b2976f8c2" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483446}}] :activity/title "Dividing With Negative Decimals and Fractions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482805}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139294} :activity/id #uuid "88d9417f-4dcb-5b07-99a4-4fc3631a5617" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048438 :topic/id #uuid "9f62517d-bdce-5662-9e1b-19d15df5109b" :topic/math-academy-id 2234} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139294 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139715 :tutorial/content "We always introduce **errors** in our measurements whenever we round or approximate numbers. In this lesson, we will explore how to quantify and describe measurement errors.\n\nSuppose that a particular rope measures precisely $48.9\\,\\textrm{cm}.$ Now, imagine we take a meterstick and record a measurement of $50\\,\\textrm{cm}$ *to the nearest $10$ centimeters*. Let's mark this approximation on our meterstick.\n\n![](images/4e/4e5d5ca05f27aa612b3fdcc9430b495870ddf540630857b6227897d6b20c9e89.png)\n\nThe error of a measurement equals the difference between the rounded estimate and the \"true\" value. So, in this example, the error of our approximation equals\n\n$$\n50\\,\\textrm{cm} - 48.9\\,\\textrm{cm} = 1.1\\textrm{cm}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nWe often don't know the \"true\" measurement in practical problems. So, the next best thing is to determine a **lower bound** and an **upper bound** for our measurement:\n\n- The *lower* bound of a measurement is its *smallest* possible value.\n\n- The *upper* bound of a measurement is its *largest* possible value.\n\nLet's figure out the lower and upper bounds of our $50\\,\\textrm{cm}$ approximation:\n\n- The *lower bound* is $45 \\, \\textrm{cm}$ because this is the *smallest* value that gives $50 \\, \\textrm{cm}$ when rounded to the nearest $10\\,\\textrm{cm}.$\n\n![](images/da/da9cd6208cef81c6fe34fbba9b4271fa3ce1f30df69eb3c631ce6fea4f12e3a3.png)\n\n- The *upper bound* is $55 \\, \\textrm{cm}$ because this is (almost) the *largest* value that gives $50 \\, \\textrm{cm}$ when rounded to the nearest $10\\,\\textrm{cm}.$\n\n![](images/23/23a195d88326b587e1864ef04e7b05f74d675f1ddc987baa2cf586af599eb4a9.png)\n\nLet's use the letter $l$ to denote the \"true\" length of the rope. Then, we can combine our lower and upper bound measurements into a single statement, as follows:\n\n$$\n45\\,\\textrm{cm} \\leq l \\lt 55\\,\\textrm{cm}\n$$\n\nNotice that we used a *strict* \"<\" inequality for the upper bound. We cannot include $55\\textrm{cm}$ in our set of values because this number rounds to $60\\,\\textrm{cm},$ not $50\\,\\textrm{cm}$.\n\nWe can visualize the lower and upper bounds on our diagram as follows:\n\n![](images/1f/1fdacb0787e8316a78da1ad65aa177e9a6f29215fa0d53aef88b0eb33326218d.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "d44fcde5-ad8a-596b-a6ac-955ff40b6bd9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8751 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "cc0c262d-dd9e-52a1-a83b-9f1c0fe96262" :step/math-academy-id 165169 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139295}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139295 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139716 :tutorial/content "So, we've seen how to calculate lower and upper bounds. Before we consider another example, note the following:\n\n- The lower and upper bound depend on our approximation's **degree of accuracy**.\n\n- In the last example, the degree of accuracy was $10\\,\\textrm{cm}$ because the rope was measured to the nearest $10$ centimeters.\n\n- If we change the degree of accuracy, the lower and upper bounds will also change. So, for example, if we measured the rope to the nearest $1\\,\\textrm{cm},$ the lower and upper bounds would be different.\n\n- The **greatest possible error** is the largest amount the \"true\" value can be from the approximation.\n\n- This greatest possible error is always one-half of the degree of accuracy. So, in the last example, the greatest possible error in our measurement was\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{10\\,\\textrm{cm}}{2} = {\\color{blue}{5\\,\\textrm{cm}}}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the greatest possible error to compute the lower and upper bounds quickly:\n\n- To compute the lower bound of an approximation, we *subtract* the greatest possible error from the approximation. So, for the previous example, we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm{lower bound} = 50\\,\\textrm{cm} - {\\color{blue}{5\\,\\textrm{cm}}} = 45\\,\\textrm{cm.}\n$$\n\n- To compute the upper bound of an approximation, we *add* the greatest possible error to the approximation. So, for the previous example, we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm{upper bound} = 50\\,\\textrm{cm} + {\\color{blue}{5\\,\\textrm{cm}}} = 55\\,\\textrm{cm.}\n$$\n\nLet's see another example." :tutorial/id #uuid "f1808067-d542-577c-a2d3-085e0fb1df59" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8752 :tutorial/title "The Greatest Possible Error"} :step/id #uuid "66ba1355-ab27-5d81-8157-a359b9801285" :step/math-academy-id 165170 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139296}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139296 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138387 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138164 :question/id #uuid "b18fef84-bbbb-58c6-a215-4146461c28f9" :question/math-academy-id "e-14084"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "61a5c363-0371-5820-a4e4-b21335406e50" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048588} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049051} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049484} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050051}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138697} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138752} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476906} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476950}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Bounds When Rounding to a Whole Number"} :step/id #uuid "bf5aa060-ae70-531a-8e1c-d165c1d25adb" :step/math-academy-id 165171 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139297}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139297 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138388 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138165 :question/id #uuid "9e0f0485-0635-558e-8493-eef61ee623f7" :question/math-academy-id "e-14085"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e99cabd4-b713-50e1-83be-f8571b575803" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050934}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138698} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138753} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476919} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476925} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536858}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Bounds When Rounding to a Decimal Place"} :step/id #uuid "7d898aa1-1cb4-5977-87b3-02ae7082146a" :step/math-academy-id 165174 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139298}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139298 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138389 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138167 :question/id #uuid "44ff13ac-1980-554e-aea8-4aaa51d202fc" :question/math-academy-id "e-14087"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "29217571-4597-5bbe-aecd-0d9a1d1aa1c1" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050415} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050436} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050792}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138737} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138801} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476974} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476975}] :knowledge-point/title "Bounding Areas and Volumes"} :step/id #uuid "761a41b0-39e8-54bb-9403-886bbe9ed521" :step/math-academy-id 165177}] :activity/title "Degrees of Accuracy" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186138593}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 660.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153298} :activity/id #uuid "b42cbc99-dc3a-5ea5-af15-2e43d6dacdce" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048502 :topic/id #uuid "a4f450cf-8fc5-50db-946a-e2e69ec67915" :topic/math-academy-id 2560} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153298 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153325 :tutorial/content "Sometimes we need to find a number that is a certain percentage *greater than* a given amount. This corresponds to applying a so-called **percentage increase.**\n\nTo apply a percentage increase, we add the increase to $100\\%,$ convert the result to a decimal multiplier, and then find the product of the multiplier and the original number.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's consider the following problem.\n\nWhat number is $25\\%$ *greater than* $160?$\n\nThe key words here are \"$\\boldsymbol{25\\%}$ **greater than.**\" This means we need to apply a percentage increase, increasing the original number by $25\\%.$\n\nTo find a number $25\\%$ greater than $160,$ we apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100\\% + 25\\% = 125\\%.\n$$\n\nThis means that our new number is $125\\%$ of $160.$ \n\nSo, first, we convert this percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n125\\% = \\dfrac{125}{100} = 1.25\n$$\n\nTo find a number that is $25\\%$ greater than $160,$ we multiply $160$ by $1.25{:}$\n\n$$\n1.25 \\cdot 160 = 200\n$$\n\nTherefore, the number that is $25\\%$ *greater than* $160$ is $200.$\n\nThis method applies to *any* percentage increase, including those greater than $100\\%{;}$ add the increase to $100\\%$ before converting to a decimal multiplier." :tutorial/id #uuid "83dbd64f-3ab9-5239-b54b-ae6d91816248" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14336 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "547ba33b-b6b3-5bae-b2dc-eacefe1e81a4" :step/math-academy-id 615692 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153299}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153299 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152295 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151899 :question/id #uuid "2cfd1ed7-8249-5970-9ddc-1bb69929951c" :question/math-academy-id "e-21450"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "125ed0b3-14ad-5af8-8370-ca6f83d93c35" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050716}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152552} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152553} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455458} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455469}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying a Percentage Increase"} :step/id #uuid "a946c7fa-5aee-54d2-b223-0a3b560b3401" :step/math-academy-id 615693 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153300}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153300 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152296 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151900 :question/id #uuid "95c3dedd-accd-57dc-91ee-4ee8fdb2f3a4" :question/math-academy-id "e-21451"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f90ea994-79f2-5409-bf96-306803b4ef01" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049467}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152554} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152555} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455451} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455478} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455495} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455498} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486395} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486445} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534011}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying a Percentage Increase: Word Problems"} :step/id #uuid "fd57418f-a99a-5c17-9192-9191eca0084b" :step/math-academy-id 615696 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153301}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153301 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153340 :tutorial/content "It's possible to increase a number by $100\\%$ or more. \n\nFor example, what number is $150\\%$ greater than $90?$\n\nTo find a number $150\\%$ *greater than* $90,$ we apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100\\% + 150\\% = 250\\%.\n$$\n\nWe begin by converting the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n250\\% = \\dfrac{250}{100} = 2.5\n$$\n\nSo, to find a number that is $150\\%$ greater than $90,$ we multiply $90$ by $2.5:$\n\n$$\n2.5 \\cdot 90 = 225\n$$\n\nTherefore, the number that is $150\\%$ greater than $90$ is $225.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "a0514684-2b6b-52ce-9ccb-6d28ed8d4850" :tutorial/math-academy-id 15090 :tutorial/title "Percentage Increases Greater Than 100%"} :step/id #uuid "a3bd6e9e-9d93-51a6-9a20-5e9292cf93f4" :step/math-academy-id 615700 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153302}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153302 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152297 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151901 :question/id #uuid "4af9bc31-4980-5c3c-9025-45e85ac09d8a" :question/math-academy-id "e-21517"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "88d0f4ad-a3f9-5275-b5d5-a9973bf63ebf" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050618}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152551} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152556} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455476} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455482} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486392} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486423} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536824}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying Percentage Increases Greater Than 100%"} :step/id #uuid "d9b4f520-6201-594a-907d-e7a899c8d57c" :step/math-academy-id 615701 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153303}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153303 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152298 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151913 :question/id #uuid "92d9c3ca-6e59-5c73-b640-114dfb7d13cd" :question/math-academy-id "e-22093"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbb0103d-d686-575a-8ef8-81640227f4dc" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152557} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152558} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455431} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455511}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving Word Problems Involving Percentage Increases Greater Than 100%"} :step/id #uuid "10eca415-b601-5bc8-80c2-c2db5a9c451e" :step/math-academy-id 615704}] :activity/title "Applying Percentage Increases" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186152315}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046648} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486523} :activity/id #uuid "332f39d2-fe5b-5fe5-bfa7-8da0dfbe5393" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048502 :topic/id #uuid "a4f450cf-8fc5-50db-946a-e2e69ec67915" :topic/math-academy-id 2560} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486464 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152558 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6a39d413-aa71-523a-b6b3-dae4d712baf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3600" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485945}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485945} :answer-field/id #uuid "290d7c72-0a37-54c1-928d-b289c4e12639" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149800}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "cf1e4b17-b634-5d17-aa2d-098e82d8022f" :question/math-academy-id "q-327654" :question/problem "A factory produced $900$ boxes of cereal last year. This year, production is $300%$ greater than last year. How many boxes of cereal did the factory produce this year?\n\nThe factory produced\n{{field-1}}\nboxes of cereal this year." :question/worked-solution "To find this year's cereal production, we apply a $300%$ increase to last year's figure.\n\nIncreasing by $300%$ means that we must apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100%+300%=400%.\n$$\n\nWe begin by converting the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n400%=\\frac{400}{100}=4\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply the original amount by $4:$\n\n$$\n900\\times 4=3,600\n$$\n\nSo, the factory produced $3600$ boxes of cereal this year."} :step/id #uuid "00e71b2a-765d-5d7f-a25b-d91d73442118"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486503 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486423 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "63a00a9b-f175-5563-a7e5-e9656e27a637" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "108" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485935} {:answer/id #uuid "758ee6d2-143b-5d0c-85c7-1b00d67826f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "120" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485969} {:answer/id #uuid "974abbd8-6ac1-5b5c-809b-ef8a1790dbd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "102" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486038} {:answer/id #uuid "aca33ef9-b028-5c98-a62b-f95962819c99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "80" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486076} {:answer/id #uuid "be2c74de-5ea2-597a-8fc7-7510c00cdf16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "95" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486103}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486038} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b3f1f35-966b-596c-862d-3ab162b9470c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486261}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "aa7ba5f1-091b-5461-b064-b9a572c0acc9" :question/math-academy-id "q-318357" :question/problem "What number is $155%$ greater than $40?$" :question/worked-solution "To find a number $155%$ *greater than* $40,$ we apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100%+155%=255%.\n$$\n\nWe begin by converting the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n255%=\\frac{255}{100}=2.55\n$$\n\nSo, to find a number that is $155%$ greater than $40,$ we multiply $40$ by $2.55:$\n\n$$\n2.55\\cdot 40=102\n$$\n\nTherefore, the number that is $155%$ greater than $40$ is $102.$"} :step/id #uuid "aa852896-f641-5bf3-81c3-1ddb33a1c5d8" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486505}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486504 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486445 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00e18203-4dbf-5257-84e1-5d825b64f9da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5,\\,000,\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485764} {:answer/id #uuid "01c47803-fa37-5ad4-a822-f030d4115e5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5,\\,050,\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485767} {:answer/id #uuid "22fa57f8-4bd0-583b-b691-d351b66f3152" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5,\\,075,\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485824} {:answer/id #uuid "26239f53-9cc0-56a7-acb2-66f332c11f19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5,\\,100,\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485828} {:answer/id #uuid "623d64e1-d70f-5584-bac1-b87f99bf2302" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5,\\,025,\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485934}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485824} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa4f452c-1a22-5679-9e96-6dcc369d76d6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486346}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ceab7d75-8909-5909-a769-ff36bb9c7667" :question/math-academy-id "q-326524" :question/problem "A country welcomed $3,\\,500,\\,000$ tourists in $2018.$  Travel authorities predict that this number will increase by $45%$ by the year $2028.$ Which of the following is closest to the projected number of tourists in $2028?$" :question/worked-solution "To find the projected number of tourists in $2028,$ we apply a $45%$ increase to the $2018$ figure.\n\nIncreasing by $45%$ means that we must apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100%+45%=145%.\n$$\n\nWe begin by converting the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n145%=\\frac{145}{100}=1.45\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute the new value by multiplying the original amount by $1.45:$\n\n$$\n3,\\,500,\\,000\\times 1.45=5,\\,075,\\,000\n$$\n\nTherefore, the projected number of tourists in $2028$ is $5,\\,075,\\,000.$"} :step/id #uuid "ad0e7bc5-f3f5-5418-b7be-7515dd52c1f2" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486464}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486505 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486395 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d2bf66f-071b-599c-870d-beb08d151c52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$$14.00$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485789} {:answer/id #uuid "81d65c1e-4fea-5d3d-b067-2a903bca796a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$$12.75$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485993} {:answer/id #uuid "9a524f5e-5406-5bdc-81e7-caf6bc6d50a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$$12.95$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486041} {:answer/id #uuid "f89dfbd4-8787-51f2-b071-d16433f03c99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$$13.50$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486213} {:answer/id #uuid "fccdd420-a0f2-5e8c-abe0-1561c37428e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$$13.14$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486226}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486226} :answer-field/id #uuid "901b558e-ee47-587e-b837-3417a076e126" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486296}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6f6e2bde-069f-5dbd-8445-437abb3c38f2" :question/math-academy-id "q-326517" :question/problem "A sandwich costs $$12$ before tax. If a $9.5%$ sales tax is applied, what is the total price after tax?" :question/worked-solution "To find the total price after tax, we apply a $9.5%$ increase to the original cost.\n\nIncreasing by $9.5%$ means that we must apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100%+9.5%=109.5%=1.095\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply the original price by $1.095:$\n\n$$\n12\\times 1.095=13.14.\n$$\n\nSo, the total price after tax is $$13.14.$"} :step/id #uuid "af48d2fc-c0ee-5116-9e15-35ee93f0388c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486504}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486523 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486392 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6a7cc659-73e4-532b-99a7-3c634766ec34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "144" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485946} {:answer/id #uuid "7b9ddf9b-1b91-530e-8514-164da8ccae4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "160" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485981} {:answer/id #uuid "b58bfefd-ae8c-59b0-b73a-de23da9e8c2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "128" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486088} {:answer/id #uuid "c94777e7-dc40-5649-ab0e-9b864344dabb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "136" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486127} {:answer/id #uuid "dc4ed7cc-6a7f-517f-944e-d4397979f27d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "180" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486166}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485946} :answer-field/id #uuid "a94424b9-97e6-5016-9825-443b370a2c58" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486306}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "62ed2686-5fd2-570f-9e2c-651f1d63d801" :question/math-academy-id "q-318127" :question/problem "What number is $125%$ greater than $64?$" :question/worked-solution "To find a number $125%$ *greater than* $64,$ we apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100%+125%=225%.\n$$\n\nWe begin by converting the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n225%=\\frac{225}{100}=2.25\n$$\n\nSo, to find a number that is $125%$ greater than $64,$ we multiply $64$ by $2.25:$\n\n$$\n2.25\\cdot 64=144\n$$\n\nTherefore, the number that is $125%$ greater than $64$ is $144.$"} :step/id #uuid "ff1f3d10-46ad-5cfc-b250-e2375bf213df" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486503}}] :activity/title "Applying Percentage Increases" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485754}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106642} :activity/id #uuid "5d527aa8-abdc-59f4-9e67-fe8dbfab8e99" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048683 :topic/id #uuid "b4904126-8a71-5d04-a71d-74c10d4620ba" :topic/math-academy-id 1289} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106632 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105742 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105599 :question/id #uuid "3ef53b20-3e82-5cd0-a1df-bd679ea8c650" :question/math-academy-id "e-4671"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbc7c6e8-63c7-5ac4-9b65-f12c006b3fde" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049714}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106469} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106470} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478377}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Power of Quotient Rule"} :step/id #uuid "2f04115d-4fb6-50da-9efd-0c167f51e86a" :step/math-academy-id 17142 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106633}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106633 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105743 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105600 :question/id #uuid "0debd4b6-66bd-5c00-8f0c-db39f5d848bc" :question/math-academy-id "e-4672"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d87a38cc-5ca0-534e-8fa1-0a6e68d65706" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106471} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106472} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478380} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493896} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536830}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Powers of Fractions Using Power of Quotient Rule"} :step/id #uuid "34836a29-5761-54fe-b6ca-a58e1fca9de8" :step/math-academy-id 17143 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106634}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106634 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105744 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105601 :question/id #uuid "80cde83c-b7fb-5ad7-bc3e-528e47151da4" :question/math-academy-id "e-4673"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "46cd136a-cc20-51e1-ac90-f8d9b787c5b3" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106473} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106474} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478376} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478425}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Powers of Negative Fractions Using the Power of Quotient Rule"} :step/id #uuid "fde68042-0dcb-5358-9469-295c9964cb49" :step/math-academy-id 17144} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106642 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107083 :tutorial/content "To raise a fraction to a power, we must raise both the numerator and the denominator to that power. This is known as the **power of quotient rule for exponents**.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's simplify the expression $\\left(\\dfrac{2}{5}\\right)^3$ by applying the power of quotient rule:\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{2}{5}\\right)^3=\\dfrac{2^3}{5^3}\n$$\n\n**Note:** The power of quotient rule for exponents is really just a quicker alternative to using repeated multiplication, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{3} & =(\\frac{2}{5})\\times (\\frac{2}{5})\\times (\\frac{2}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\times 2\\times 2}{5\\times 5\\times 5} \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{3}}{{5}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "8abc3ed3-0ba8-5dc1-8bf8-437985614b92" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3273 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "c9e8e3ef-fed3-5e60-ae33-e12940a91210" :step/math-academy-id 17997 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106632}}] :activity/title "The Power of Quotient Rule for Exponents" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105965}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046648} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494040} :activity/id #uuid "7e7b6dc7-4114-5b93-bb27-c0eeaa8b140f" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048683 :topic/id #uuid "b4904126-8a71-5d04-a71d-74c10d4620ba" :topic/math-academy-id 1289} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493993 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493896 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36fd9457-00f5-537f-ad12-5edc30b6be51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493479} {:answer/id #uuid "3fcd1bb2-1500-5ee2-b804-b199a4af59f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493499} {:answer/id #uuid "72f3cf98-f8a7-5839-b0ee-742e6ecc2ac4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493561} {:answer/id #uuid "da258386-0ee6-57ce-b6d3-41ef50f8b2d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493733} {:answer/id #uuid "f175e3a1-caba-5bec-9be2-4f528d2943b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493781}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493499} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8aa62db-1655-53a1-a21b-36bf03df3896" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493887}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "151ea9a2-7fca-5107-a3ac-b15fd8b562e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-68461" :question/problem "${(\\frac{1}{2})}^{3}=$" :question/worked-solution "To raise the fraction to the power of $3$, we must raise both the numerator and the denominator to the power of $3:$\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{3}=\\frac{{1}^{3}}{{2}^{3}}\n$$\n\nNow, using, ${1}^{3}=1$ and ${2}^{3}=8,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{{1}^{3}}{{2}^{3}}=\\frac{1}{8}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "341ef616-a07f-5fa1-b233-8905f2e7e196" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494048}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494040 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106471 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fe24bada-d3e5-58d4-87be-b638964a837f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104975} {:answer/id #uuid "42c68a77-9d73-5bab-93cb-6b7a5e748fac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104976} {:answer/id #uuid "55d4e952-a6d8-5904-946b-38407b47e342" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104977} {:answer/id #uuid "5ddacffd-3676-5cad-8eda-6c8155b58081" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104978} {:answer/id #uuid "6d66a109-c2bd-5416-af13-846d5afe3a77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104979}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186104977} :answer-field/id #uuid "1fc66f7f-3850-5fa3-bc61-13386480b4f3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104974}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5b2c788a-e2e8-529d-bb25-574b06c58bb0" :question/math-academy-id "q-68458" :question/problem "${(\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}=$" :question/worked-solution "To raise the fraction to the power of $2$, we must raise both the numerator and the denominator to the power of $2:$\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}=\\frac{{3}^{2}}{{5}^{2}}\n$$\n\nNow, using ${3}^{2}=9$ and ${5}^{2}=25,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{{3}^{2}}{{5}^{2}}=\\frac{9}{25}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "9adbfcf0-8452-52d5-b4fb-e01320e32a18" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493993}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494048 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106473 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9f287d7b-8e45-585a-98fa-28040508099a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104987} {:answer/id #uuid "ce55da7f-50bc-50e1-97d4-2fb77506d6e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{49}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104988} {:answer/id #uuid "f9e6c57d-980f-5bf5-abe5-039788ff6f9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104989} {:answer/id #uuid "3a096c69-a948-55c4-b1f3-408814661ef8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104990} {:answer/id #uuid "4f053517-545a-56dd-a930-55fa0e6815e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{49}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104991}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186104988} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9230a8b-5c46-59a1-bb96-933a3909004f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104986}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e207c000-8554-5f94-b1da-034972cbe6db" :question/math-academy-id "q-68467" :question/problem "${(-\\frac{1}{7})}^{2}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $-\\frac{1}{7}=\\frac{(-1)}{7}.$\n\nTo raise the fraction to the power of $2$, we must raise both the numerator and the denominator to the power of $2:$\n\n$$\n{(-\\frac{1}{7})}^{2}={(\\frac{(-1)}{7})}^{2}=\\frac{(-1)^{2}}{{7}^{2}}\n$$\n\nNow, using $(-1)^{2}=1$ and ${7}^{2}=49,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{(-1)^{2}}{{7}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{49}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "b0856f94-0f86-5940-aa64-f087a7c6407e"}] :activity/title "The Power of Quotient Rule for Exponents" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493379}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065135} :activity/id #uuid "457bb695-c5a7-5c4b-bc54-87bb84576ab7" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048957 :topic/id #uuid "cf262221-31bc-5e58-a6f2-8acf1c2b4906" :topic/math-academy-id 355} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065135 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065367 :tutorial/content "Fractions that contain multiple terms in the numerator and a single term in the denominator can be split into separate terms.\n\nFor example, \n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\dfrac {x + 2} {3} &= \\\n\t\t\\dfrac x 3 + \\dfrac 2 3\n\t}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\dfrac {3a - 4b + 1} {5} &= \\\n\t\t\\dfrac {3a} 5 - \\dfrac {4b} 5 + \\dfrac 1 5.\n\t}\n$$\n\nOften, we're able to simplify the fractions in the individual terms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\dfrac {10x + 9} {2} &= \\\n\t\t\\dfrac {10x} {2} + \\dfrac 9 2 &= \\\n\t\t\\dfrac {10} {2} \\cdot x + \\dfrac 9 2 &= \\\t\n\t\t5 \\cdot x + \\dfrac 9 2 &= \\\n\t\t5x + \\dfrac 9 2 \n\t}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "0a37b6ec-6750-5aea-ae26-ad82d059a0ef" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3375 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "871a576b-9882-5cab-a267-5bfaae09bb5c" :step/math-academy-id 18870 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065136}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065136 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064257 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064102 :question/id #uuid "6e148f32-5352-5368-a08a-6da278a6068d" :question/math-academy-id "e-5124"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "724bdf2f-c9f0-564e-ae